invariant explicitly asserts required-module graph per class. Do not claim it catches “visual audit” findings unless PDFs/images are also rendered and inspected by automated predicates.\n\n- **W0 — plug-in direction correct, but over-scoped and overclaimed**\n  - **Placement:** Correct loci: G1b, Generator schema, Phase 2 grammar gates, Engine B/D.\n  - **Architectural conflict:** “Replaces scattered registries” is dangerous. First implement object model as **canonical source + compatibility adapters**. Do not delete registries until outputs are byte/semantic-equivalent on harness.\n  - **Overclaim:** “Generator can’t emit axial fan at 150 Pa because type system rejects it” is false unless:\n    - Generator output is structured JSON against schema.\n    - There is validation before R1.\n    - Invalid output causes repair/retry/fail.\n  - **Value claim:**  \n    - K10 bleed: yes, if object graph drives required links.  \n    - Duplicate BoM rows: maybe, only if object identity/canonical BoM IDs exist.  \n    - LED 10× mismatch: not type-checkable unless load/driver equations are encoded.  \n    - Fan stall: not type-checkable unless fan curve constraints are encoded.  \n  - So “4/10 prevented at type-check time” is overclaim. More accurate: **K10 definitely; duplicate rows likely; LED/fan only after W1-style constraints.**\n\n- **W1 — plug-in plausible, but Phase 1 function-calling is high-risk**\n  - **Placement:** Good dual mode: in-flight tools + post-hoc gates. But start post-hoc first.\n  - **Conflict:** Gemini 3.1 Pro’s current prompt likely expects free-form module decomposition. Tool calling can change output shape, token flow, and latency. Wrap it behind a feature flag.\n  - **Value claim:**  \n    - Fan stall: yes, with fan curve lookup.  \n    - RO pump pressure: only if you have pump curve + pipe pressure + required head, not just `pressure_drop.pipe`.  \n    - LED driver mismatch: not covered by listed VF micro tools unless electrical/load sizing tool is included. Voltage drop alone does not catch 10× driver mismatch.\n  - Step 3 proof is measurable if defined as: `count(HIGH findings with tags in {fan_static, pump_head, led_driver_load}) / run`.\n\n- **W4 — plug-in okay, value weak**\n  - **Placement:** Pre-merge CI is right.\n  - **Issue:** “2 LLMs steel-man before + 2 red-team after” is expensive/noisy and not deterministic.\n  - **Value claim:** Name collision was caught by type-check. Claiming red-team would have caught it is weak. Better value claim: catches semantic regressions not covered by typecheck/golden invariants.\n  - Proof should be mutation tests: seed known bad patches and measure red-team recall.\n\n- **W3 — plug-in direction right, replacement is risky**\n  - **Placement:** R1-R4 is the correct region.\n  - **Architectural conflict:** Do not replace all generic reviewers initially. Run specialists **in parallel** or replace only R2/R3 for target modules.\n  - **Risk:** Losing Qwen 3.6 Max as a broad generalist may reduce cross-module synthesis, weirdness detection, and non-domain bugs. Specialists tunnel-vision.\n  - **Value claim:** Electrical expert likely catches LED mismatch if prompt/checklist/tool explicitly covers load-driver relationship. Without a deterministic electrical tool, still not guaranteed.\n\n- **W7 — plug-in correct, value overclaimed**\n  - **Placement:** Pre-generator retrieval is right; also feed W3 specialists.\n  - **Conflict:** Exemplar injection can cause copying, stale standards, wrong jurisdiction, and context bloat.\n  - **Value claim:** Engine B under-pricing would not necessarily be prevented. Engine B happens later and may override/estimate prices. Exemplars may improve magnitude, but “would emit prices right from start” depends on generator owning price fields. More honest: reduces scale/template errors and gives Engine B better component/spec anchors.\n\n- **W5 — plug-in correct**\n  - **Placement:** Offline PR-producing batch is correct. Human-gated is essential.\n  - **Value claim:** Good, but “after 20 VF runs” is not automatic unless telemetry captures normalized component class, estimated price, final corrected price, gate failures, and manual overrides.\n\n- **W2 — plug-in partially wrong**\n  - **Placement:** Between K10 and Headline catches design-vs-reference mismatch, but the cited failure was **rendered image mismatch**. That can happen after state finalization.\n  - Better placement:\n    - Design grounding after K10.\n    - Render grounding after `render-minimal-pdf.tsx` produces image/page metadata, before upload.\n  - **Value claim:** Cabinet-image-on-container only caught if W2 sees actual selected/rendered hero image, not just module list/reference images.\n\n- **W6-full — plug-in correct**\n  - **Placement:** Telemetry + dashboard outside chain is right.\n  - **Value claim:** Accurate if model/version/class/run metadata are recorded. “Quality drops” requires stable scoring rubric, not just changing council opinions.\n\n---\n\n## C) Rollout proof points\n\n- Step 3 is measurable/falsifiable **only if normalized**:\n  - Fixed 5 VF briefs.\n  - Same model versions or recorded model drift.\n  - Same critic prompt/rubric.\n  - Findings tagged by category/severity.\n  - Metric: `HIGH physics findings per run`, plus targeted tags.\n  - Pass criterion predeclared, e.g. `>=50% reduction in targeted HIGH findings and no increase in total blocked runs`.\n- “Council confirms got better” is not enough. Council should adjudicate blinded before/after outputs and produce structured labels.\n\n---\n\n## D) Biggest risks\n\n- **W0 wrong type system**\n  - Breaks generator output, gates, suppliers, pricing, render assumptions.\n  - Could silently remove valid class-specific modules.\n  - Mitigation: adapters, feature flag, VF-only first, dual-run old/new object model, diff object graph outputs.\n\n- **W1 function-calling**\n  - Can destabilize Gemini’s generation format.\n  - Tool latency/caching/failure modes can cause partial designs.\n  - Start with post-hoc gate replacement, then limited in-flight calls for one module.\n\n- **W3 replacing R1-R4**\n  - Losing generalist diversity is real. Keep one generalist reviewer after specialists for integration/sanity.\n\n- **W6-lite 7 hrs**\n  - Not viable as every-commit blocking CI. Use smoke/full/nightly tiers, sharding, cached supplier/price calls, and budget limits.\n\n---\n\n## E) Missing\n\n- Feature flags + rollback plan per W.\n- Stable telemetry schema.\n- Model/version pinning.\n- Golden brief selection/adjudication rubric.\n- Cost/latency budgets per step.\n- Mutation-test suite with known bad patches.\n- Render-level visual validation for W2.\n- Human review protocol: who approves failed harness diffs and why.\n- Backward compatibility plan for existing state.json consumers.\n\n---\n\n## F) Start point\n\nStart with **W6-lite**, but not the full 15-brief monster as blocking CI.\n\nRecommended first step:\n\n1. Build replay harness + invariant framework.\n2. Add 3 smoke golden briefs, one per class.\n3. Capture baseline state/PDF artifacts.\n4. Add nightly 15-brief run.\n5. Only then start W0/W1.\n\nReason: without the harness, W0 and W1 are high-blast-radius edits with no reliable regression detector.","refusal":null,"reasoning":null,"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.encrypted","data":"gAAAAABqDXD2YbHg2O61nvyQw_Y2xEtO0fs-EgtzfiuDijak1LF51E-V7TdknU_9hmMglsM9aV0KY53KGZ766Erx1RM4Db9KN_9yMQsC3UpjIrPMFn6vO0L96U9KDqbm6UpAZugS2pzmiUmC80dMHbtCitiPzwHJAJdiWlSPKtrPbbVKQ5VEkitoKPKDmZf-5jCEvSWC4BRBztM9fjpXLw68wA1tjbJwA-yTcd472I7rJpe-z3ttCTUazqJS_01MHPA4gmOy4mtrj5JTmRWP9tnTN7bnx6eSJN1qwyImpATHtg3m3cbVJZCm-SjzH2fVydp3rAo_S398FuyDnqYBYrxv6W97OaR6cJ-G2NHinEixkFNHnU2p7XO3QcXk_GVW1b_t_oktgzODohId2mn5ospy3yxFwhbIzbh8fEuJbYSUIQcXaJ3OSpchnEhE5izoKMJdHRPnDvG9CwRQm_Md8f-xTNFYy-nEobZWYvMlc_kOXP_zxzFc7pOTe30JS7cgjQXE2S77MPR9rfuHFgMjuPZsh9rcNlXwY_Maw-wR4L60nWNwMZyzXeoD9xcVDRsHDMOON5HvrEIsD6E_LhpIGp7bYfC27wFEG8Wmrzd3hBSkugeAFo05JQvX537I1pxkOiUgXcoJ